    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Positive transverse photon one-particle space",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the real momentum dot product and norm are those of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Explicitly extend the real dot product\ncomplex-bilinearly to $a\\cdot b=\\sum_{j=1}^3a_jb_j$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb C^3$.\nEach complex fiber instead has the positive Hermitian pairing\n$\\langle a,b\\rangle_2=\\overline a\\cdot b=\\sum_j\\overline a_jb_j$ and\nnorm $\\|a\\|^2=\\sum_j|a_j|^2$; the displayed integral uses this pairing.\nHilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T} =\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-complex-dimensional plane $\\{a\\in\\mathbb C^3:\\mathbf k\\cdot a=0\\}$ perpendicular to the real vector $\\mathbf k$ in that Hermitian pairing; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §§6.2.1–6.2.2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; Coulomb-gauge transverse photon modes and the subsidiary condition."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 book draft, §§54–56",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page book draft; Maxwell modes, Coulomb gauge and the transverse projector."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Charged gauge data and SI-natural conventions",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Use the fixed Minkowski coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)=(ct,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, on an open coordinate domain $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ with its standard topology. Each $x^a$ has unit metre. For a smooth scalar component $u:U\\to\\mathbb R$ or $u:U\\to\\mathbb C$, define $\\partial_a u:=\\partial u/\\partial x^a$; thus $\\partial_0=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ at fixed $\\mathbf x$, $\\partial_j=\\partial/\\partial x^j$ for $j=1,2,3$, and each $\\partial_a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Raise derivative indices with $\\partial^a:=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_b$. The fields in this definition are smooth maps\n$$\\phi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R),\\qquad \\mathbf A\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^3),\\qquad \\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4),\\qquad \\chi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R).$$\nDefine the lower-potential component maps $A_a:U\\to\\mathbb R$ by $A_0=-\\phi/c$ and $A_j=\\mathbf A_j$; collectively $(A_a)$ is the real covector potential in this chart. Then $F_{ab}:=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ has smooth real component maps $F_{ab}:U\\to\\mathbb R$. The spinor is an ordinary commuting $\\mathbb C^4$-valued map, not a tuple of Grassmann generators. Its Dirac adjoint is the smooth row-valued map $\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta:U\\to(\\mathbb C^4)^*$, using the fixed matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$. In fixed SI units, $\\phi$ is in volts, each $A_a$ is in $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, $\\psi$ is assigned field dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, and $\\chi$ is in webers; these unit labels accompany the maps and do not identify $q$ with a measured charge. For periodic spatial domains, the same component and unit conventions apply to smooth periodic coordinate lifts, with the quotient domain specified by the item using them.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nHere $D_a$ acts on $\\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$ by $(D_a\\psi)(x)=\\partial_a\\psi(x)-ig a_a(x)\\psi(x)$, again a smooth map $U\\to\\mathbb C^4$. Thus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor the real smooth gauge-parameter map $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R$ in webers, put $\\alpha:=\\chi/S_0\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §§6.1–6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; QED gauge transformations, photon polarizations, Coulomb constraint and field observables."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 book draft, §§54–58",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page book draft; Maxwell and Coulomb gauge, transverse modes, and QED conventions. Its signature and charge conventions are translated explicitly here."
            },
            {
              "title": "BIPM, The International System of Units (SI), 9th edition, version 4.01",
              "url": "https://www.bipm.org/documents/20126/41483022/SI-Brochure-9.pdf",
              "locator": "Official brochure §§2.2 and 2.3.1; exact fixed constants and the experimentally determined status of $\\mu_0$."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-photon-no-global-polarization-frame",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "No global continuous real polarization frame",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Local A-page prerequisite added because the two-polarization example requires the no-global-frame result; complete proof reduces it to the published even-sphere theorem.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "thm-no-nowhere-zero-tangent-vector-field-on-an-even-sphere"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-no-nowhere-zero-tangent-vector-field-on-an-even-sphere": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. For $n\\in S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, let $E_n=n^\\perp=\\operatorname{Ran}(I-nn^T)$ be the real transverse plane. There do not exist continuous maps $e_1,e_2:S^2\\to\\mathbb R^3$ such that, for every $n$, $(e_1(n),e_2(n))$ is an orthonormal basis of $E_n$. A continuous real orthonormal polarization frame would in particular give a continuous nowhere-zero tangent vector field on $S^2$.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Algebraic Topology, Theorem 2.28, p.135",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATch2.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted chapter 2; Theorem 2.28 proves that even-dimensional spheres have no nowhere-zero tangent vector field."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §6.2.2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; photon polarization vectors and transverse modes."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-photon-null-quotient",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Lorenz auxiliary subsidiary null quotient",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use $\\mathcal H_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. On $\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^4)$ define\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger\\eta\\,\\delta(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,\\qquad \\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1).$$\nFor $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, let $n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and set\n$$\\mathcal K_0=\\{\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K:-\\epsilon^0+n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=0\\text{ a.e.}\\},\\qquad T\\epsilon=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-n\\epsilon^0.$$\nThen $\\mathcal K_0$ is closed, $T:\\mathcal K_0\\to\\mathcal H_T$ is bounded and onto, and\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\langle T\\epsilon,T\\delta\\rangle_2\\quad(\\epsilon,\\delta\\in\\mathcal K_0).$$\nIts radical (the vectors pairing to zero with all of $\\mathcal K_0$) is\n$$\\mathcal N=\\ker T=\\{b(\\mathbf k)(1,n(\\mathbf k)):b\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3)\\}.$$\nConsequently $\\mathcal K_0/\\mathcal N$ with the induced form is a positive Hilbert space isometric to $\\mathcal H_T$. The form on the four-component auxiliary space is indefinite; only the subsidiary quotient is the positive physical one-particle space.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §§6.2.1–6.2.2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; covariant auxiliary photon modes, subsidiary condition, negative norm and null quotient."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 book draft, §§54–56",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page book draft; covariant photon modes, Coulomb gauge and polarization space."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Smeared Coulomb photon operator distribution",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $j=(j_1,j_2,j_3)\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^4;\\mathbb R^3)$ and use $\\mathcal H_T$ and $P_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. For $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$ and $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, define\n$$g_j(\\mathbf k)=\\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{3/2}(2\\omega)^{1/2}}\\, P_T(\\mathbf k)\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}j(x)e^{i\\omega x^0-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\,d^4x.$$\nThen $g_j\\in\\mathcal H_T$. On the finite-particle domain $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ of the bosonic Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$, the smeared transverse potential is\n$$A_T(j)=a(g_j)+a^\\dagger(g_j).$$\nIts matrix elements are continuous in the test-function topology; it is an operator-valued distribution and does not assign a pointwise operator to $A_i(x)$. With $E_i=-\\partial_0A_i$, the equal-time canonical commutator is the distribution\n$$[A_i(0,\\mathbf x),E_j(0,\\mathbf y)] =-i\\,P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla_{\\mathbf x})\\delta^3(\\mathbf x-\\mathbf y).$$",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §§6.2.1–6.2.2",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; transverse mode expansion and Coulomb-gauge photon field."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 book draft, §§54–56",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page book draft; canonical transverse photon modes and Coulomb gauge."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-photon-transverse-projector-kernel",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Newtonian off-diagonal kernel of the transverse projector",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Local A-page prerequisite added because the Coulomb-potential counterexample needs the Fourier projector kernel; complete proof derives it by Abel regularization and identifies its Newtonian off-diagonal part.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments",
          "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "thm-chain-rule",
          "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac",
          "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-em-sphere-zonal-moments": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-dominated-convergence": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-minus-laplacian-of-the-fundamental-solution-is-dirac": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $G(\\mathbf x)=1/(4\\pi|\\mathbf x|)$ on $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, interpreted as its locally integrable distribution, and use the Fourier convention\n$$\\widehat f(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}f(\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x,\\qquad f(\\mathbf x)=(2\\pi)^{-3}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\widehat f(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k.$$\nThen $\\mathcal F^{-1}(|\\mathbf k|^{-2})=G$ distributionally. Consequently, for $P_{T,ij}(\\mathbf k)=\\delta_{ij}-k_i k_j/|\\mathbf k|^2$,\n$$P_{T,ij}(-i\\nabla)\\delta^3 =\\delta_{ij}\\delta^3+\\partial_i\\partial_jG.$$\nFor $\\mathbf x\\ne0$,\n$$\\partial_i\\partial_jG(\\mathbf x) =-\\frac{\\delta_{ij}}{4\\pi|\\mathbf x|^3} +\\frac{3x_ix_j}{4\\pi|\\mathbf x|^5}.$$\nThus the equal-time transverse-potential commutator of [[lem-qft-photon-smeared-potential]] has this off-diagonal Newtonian kernel (with its stated factor $-i$). The identity is distributional at the origin; the displayed classical derivative formula is only asserted away from it.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §6.2.1 Coulomb Gauge",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; equations 6.517–6.519 give the transverse projector commutator."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted 2006 book draft, §55 Electrodynamics in Coulomb Gauge",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page book draft; equations 55.3–55.4 and 55.20 give the transverse projector and equal-time commutator. Conventions are translated explicitly here."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-dirac-gauge-current",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Dirac gauge covariance, action and conserved current",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
