      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Gauss multiplier and smooth periodic Coulomb solver",
        "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-track.md#charged-gauge-data-and-si-natural-conventions",
        "design_state": "Assigned QFT-MG01 research interface authored as a conditional mathematical item with exact hypotheses and a complete local proof.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, with $t$ measured in seconds and $x^0=ct$, and let $T_L^3=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^3$ be the flat spatial torus with period $L>0$ metres and periodic coordinates $\\mathbf x$. Take jointly smooth periodic maps\n$$\\mathbf A:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad \\phi:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\rho:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\mathbf J:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R^3.$$\nWrite $\\dot{\\mathbf A}:=\\partial_t\\mathbf A$ for the time partial derivative at fixed $\\mathbf x$, and $\\mathbf B:=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$. All spatial differential operators below are the periodic coordinate derivatives, and each displayed spatial integral and Hamiltonian is evaluated at a fixed $t\\in I$; the time argument is suppressed in the formulas. In SI units, $\\mathbf A$ has unit $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, $\\phi$ has unit $\\mathrm V$, $\\rho$ has unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-3}}$, and $\\mathbf J$ has unit $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$. Thus $\\dot{\\mathbf A}$ and $\\nabla\\phi$ both have unit $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{T\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and every term of the formal classical Lagrangian density has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. Let $c>0$ have unit $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, let $\\mu_0>0$ be the symbolic magnetic constant with unit $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$, and put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ with unit $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$; no numerical value for these constants is adopted. Define the formal classical density\n$$\\mathcal L=\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}|\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi|^2-\\frac1{2\\mu_0}|\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A|^2-\\rho\\phi+\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A.$$\nThe canonical momenta are $\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\epsilon_0(\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi)$, with unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-2}}$, and $\\pi_\\phi=0$. Periodic integration by parts gives the fixed-time Hamiltonian\n$$H(t)=\\int_{T_L^3}\\left(\\frac{|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2}{2\\epsilon_0}+\\frac{|\\mathbf B|^2}{2\\mu_0}+\\phi(\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho)-\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A\\right)d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nVariation of the multiplier $\\phi$ imposes $\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho=0$. In Coulomb gauge $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf A=0$, this becomes $-\\Delta\\phi=\\rho/\\epsilon_0$. At each fixed $t$, a smooth periodic solution can exist only if $\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$. If $\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$ for every $t\\in I$, there is a unique jointly smooth periodic solution with zero spatial mean, whose Fourier coefficients are\n$$\\widehat\\phi_0(t)=0,\\qquad \\widehat\\phi_k(t)=\\frac{\\widehat\\rho_k(t)}{\\epsilon_0|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\quad(k\\ne0),\\qquad \\widehat\\rho_k(t)=L^{-3}\\int_{[0,L]^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)e^{-2\\pi i k\\cdot\\mathbf x/L}\\,d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nFor every $t\\in I$ it satisfies\n$$\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}\\int_{T_L^3}|\\nabla\\phi|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf x=\\frac12\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho\\phi\\,d^3\\mathbf x=\\frac{L^3}{2\\epsilon_0}\\sum_{k\\ne0}\\frac{|\\widehat\\rho_k(t)|^2}{|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\ge0.$$\nThis is a classical conditional solver for smooth periodic neutral data; it makes no claim about quantum density products or nonperiodic boundary conditions.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML chapter 6; Coulomb gauge and the Gauss-law constraint."
